      "Language": "ger",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
          "content": "The fear-avoidance model (FAM) has gained widespread acceptance as a conceptual framework predicting chronic pain development. However, there is still a high amount of unexplained variance in the prediction of outcome variables. This study aimed to test depression as a mediator of prospective links between the FAM variables pain intensity, pain-related fear, avoidance behavior and disability in order to achieve a better prediction of model variables."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIAL AND METHODS",
          "content": "Single mediation analyses were conducted using path analysis of longitudinal data from patients with chronic back pain (n = 243)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "There was at least partial mediation by depression for all prospective relations of FAM variables analyzed. The mediation effects increased the percentage of explained variance of the predicted model variables."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The findings provide support for additional consideration of depressive symptoms to obtain a better understanding of prospective associations between FAM variables. The findings indicate that in the treatment of chronic back pain following a multiple target approach to reduce pain, pain-related fear and avoidance behavior and also depressive symptoms should be considered."
        }
      ]},
